            <ArticleTitle>The relationship between coronary plaque characteristics and small embolic particles during coronary stent implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1635-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We investigated the relationship between coronary plaque components and small embolic particles during stenting and examined the influence on the coronary microcirculation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In vivo tissue characterization of atherosclerotic plaques was introduced by the Virtual Histology intravascular ultrasound (VH-IVUS) system (Volcano Therapeutics, Inc., Rancho Cordova, California).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study consisted of 44 patients who underwent elective coronary stenting. Plaque characteristics were identified with VH-IVUS, and small embolic particles liberated during stenting were detected as high-intensity transient signals (HITS) with a Doppler guidewire. Coronary flow velocity reserve (CFVR) was also measured before and after stenting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients were divided into the tertiles according to the HITS counts: the lowest, HITS &lt;5 (n = 16); the middle, 5 to 12 (n = 15); and the highest, &gt;12 (n = 13). Dense calcium and necrotic core area identified with VH-IVUS were significantly larger in the highest tertile (lowest vs. middle vs. highest; dense calcium: 0.2 +/- 0.3 mm2 vs. 0.3 +/- 0.6 mm2 vs. 0.8 +/- 0.7 mm2, p = 0.007; necrotic core: 0.5 +/- 0.4 mm2 vs. 0.9 +/- 0.9 mm2 vs. 1.8 +/- 1.0 mm2, p &lt; 0.001, respectively). Multivariate logistic regression analysis revealed only necrotic core area was an independent predictor of high HITS counts (odds ratio 4.41, p = 0.045). Furthermore, there was a significant negative correlation between the HITS count and CFVR after stenting (r = -0.35, p = 0.017).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The necrotic core component identified with VH-IVUS is related to liberation of small embolic particles during coronary stenting, which results in the poorer recovery of CFVR.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
